{"parentUuid":"7f8e1027-ece5-446e-ab9f-ced40beb2e46","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a36d33351616095f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016tQ1cgWXEK6U3nd7rMC8C2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B2W8KCNcLuyPda7YbWpiwG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n -E \"subsumption|trie|dict.*cache|cross.*candidate|cut.*pool|memoization|specific.*recommend|建议|预期收益|实施\" /home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd/docs/research/agent_transcripts/ae3c21075cb388b14.output","description":"Find recommendations and definitions in transcript"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7138,"cache_read_input_tokens":19842,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7138,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":52,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CasSDPLd8ifXW5MYysfC1","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4b31816b-6f81-4bc4-9f5f-d938506babaf","timestamp":"2026-05-09T18:03:35.754Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"4b31816b-6f81-4bc4-9f5f-d938506babaf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a8f1a005-6197-4ac8-b6c3-3f7619cec3c7","agentId":"a36d33351616095f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B2W8KCNcLuyPda7YbWpiwG","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (35.9KB). Full output saved to: /home/zhuran24/.claude/projects/-home-zhuran24-claude-pj-zmd/7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9/tool-results/bp5uqp55p.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n1:{\"parentUuid\":null,\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215\",\"agentId\":\"ae3c21075cb388b14\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"调研 CP/SAT 求解中子问题解的缓存（memoization / subproblem caching）和跨候选复用的方法。\\n\\n**背景：** 我们项目用 LBBD：master 给一个布局，binding/routing/flow 子问题分别验证。**关键观察**：相似的 master 布局会反复触发相似的子问题求解。如果能缓存子问题的解（feasible / infeasible certificate），跨 master 调用复用，能省下大量重复求解。\\n\\n**调研重点（用 WebSearch + WebFetch）：**\\n\\n1. **CP/MIP 子问题缓存的学术工作**：\\n   - Subproblem caching 在 LBBD 上的应用\\n   - 缓存 key 设计（怎么判断两个子问题\\\"等价\\\"）\\n   - 缓存命中率经验数据\\n\\n2. **不只是完全相同，还有\\\"包含/被包含\\\"匹配**：\\n   - 如果新子问题是已缓存子问题的\\\"超集 INFEASIBLE\\\"，可立即得 INFEASIBLE\\n   - subset matching / dominance 缓存\\n   - 算法复杂度\\n\\n3. **结构化缓存（normalized representation）**：\\n   - 把子问题规范化（sort variables、canonical labeling）才能匹配\\n   - bliss/nauty 等图同构工具\\n   - 工程开销\\n\\n4. **Constraint store reuse**：\\n   - 把 routing 子问题的 propagator 保留（只换初始 assumption）\\n   - SAT incremental solving\\n   - 我们项目当前是不是每次都重建 model\\n\\n5. **OR-Tools CP-SAT 的 model reuse 能力**：\\n   - 同一个 CpModel 实例多次 Solve 能否保留 learned clauses\\n   - clone CpModel 加新 hint 是否快\\n   - 工程实践\\n\\n6. **跨 master 候选的子问题共享**：\\n   - 我们 8 个硬候选都涉及类似的 binding 配置\\n   - 一个候选学到\\\"端口集 X 不可路由\\\"，其他候选能否复用\\n   - 数据结构\\n\\n7. **Persistent learned clause databases**：\\n   - 把 SAT learned clauses 写成持久数据库\\n   - 跨 session 跨候选 import\\n   - SAT 圈有没有标准格式\\n\\n8. **业界实战 - CP-SAT memoization**：\\n   - 有没有人在 OR-Tools 上做过这件事\\n   - GitHub issues / Stack Overflow\\n   - 性能数据\\n\\n9. **复杂度 vs 收益权衡**：\\n   - 缓存维护成本（内存、查找、序列化）\\n   - 命中率 X% 时净加速\\n   - 何时缓存反而拖慢\\n\\n10. **应用到我们项目的具体设计**：\\n    - 缓存什么：binding 子问题的 (port_set, ghost_rect) → result\\n    - 缓存形式：dict / SQLite / pickle\\n    - 跨进程共享：file lock / shared memory\\n    - 工程量\\n\\n**输出格式（不超过 1000 字）：**\\n- 每个调研点一段\\n- 关键论文链接 markdown 格式\\n- 末尾给\\\"对我们项目的具体实施建议 + 预期收益\\\"\\n\\n要具体到算法名、工程量。\"},\"uuid\":\"72199b17-8271-49df-a517-4274505ab268\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-07T20:41:07.779Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"D:\\\\claude pj\\\\zmd\",\"sessionId\":\"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e5c8baf6-7cb0-4265-bef4-62d07ea6487b","timestamp":"2026-05-09T18:03:35.786Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4b31816b-6f81-4bc4-9f5f-d938506babaf","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e5c8baf6-7cb0-4265-bef4-62d07ea6487b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a36d33351616095f1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01GGwMuDzsjWGwnyRTE5onxX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012uRDdNVAPBjDKrj3sFX4yL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"tail -c 30000 /home/zhuran24/.claude/projects/-home-zhuran24-claude-pj-zmd/7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9/tool-results/bp5uqp55p.txt | head -c 28000","description":"Get the recommendations section from transcript grep"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1970,"cache_read_input_tokens":26980,"output_tokens":193,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1970},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":193,"cache_read_input_tokens":26980,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1970,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CasSDypo1jERkks8FocGr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6a868e67-0d3c-435d-b014-97e732114c2d","timestamp":"2026-05-09T18:03:38.976Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6a868e67-0d3c-435d-b014-97e732114c2d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a8f1a005-6197-4ac8-b6c3-3f7619cec3c7","agentId":"a36d33351616095f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012uRDdNVAPBjDKrj3sFX4yL","type":"tool_result","content":"3-030-22788-3_1\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"[1910.11944] Logic-based Benders decomposition for large-scale optimization\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.11944\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Computational Evaluation of Cut-Strengthening Techniques in Logic-Based Benders’ Decomposition | Operations Research Forum | Springer Nature Link\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43069-023-00242-3\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/logic-based-benders-decomposition-lbbd-4e47b3ae-690a-44a4-9d80-a191b385b4e6\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Stochastic Planning and Scheduling with Logic-Based Benders Decomposition | INFORMS Journal on Computing\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/ijoc.2022.1184\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"ULD Build-Up Scheduling with Logic-Based Benders Decomposition | Springer Nature Link\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-60597-0_17\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Logic-based Benders Decomposition for Large-scale Optimization J. N. Hooker\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/LBBDbookChapter.pdf\\\"}]\\n\\nI've performed the web search on your query. The results provide substantial information about logic-based Benders decomposition and its related concepts.\\n\\n## Key Findings:\\n\\n**Core Concept:**\\nLogic-based Benders decomposition (LBBD) is a substantial generalization of classical Benders decomposition that allows the subproblem to be any optimization problem rather than specifically a linear or nonlinear programming problem.\\n\\n**No-Good Cuts:**\\nThe simplest Benders cuts are known as no-good cuts which originate with the work of Balas and Jeroslow on unit hypercubes. Logical deductions from solving a constraint program can be used to generate cuts in the form of no-good inequalities for addition to a mathematical optimisation problem, though they are typically weak—causing slow convergence of the LBBD scheme.\\n\\n**Subproblem Solutions and Cuts:**\\nLBBD derives a cut from the inference dual of the subproblem, which is the problem of finding the tightest bound on the optimal value that can be logically deduced from the constraints, with the dual solution being a proof of this bound.\\n\\n**Cut Strengthening:**\\nIt is critical for computational performance that cuts are strengthened, but the strengthening of cuts comes at the cost of solving additional subproblems. Cut-strengthening techniques that generate irreducible cuts outperform the greedy algorithm and the use of no cut strengthening.\\n\\nThe search results don't specifically address caching or memoization techniques for LBBD subproblems, though they discuss various approaches to managing and optimizing the cut generation process.\\n\\n\\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"4f4adca0-e534-4e22-83f9-422fff3ab8f4\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-07T20:41:23.377Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"9adca94a-d6cd-4715-88eb-d9433d8c204f\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"D:\\\\claude pj\\\\zmd\",\"sessionId\":\"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\",\"version\":\"2.1.128\",\"gitBranch\":\"master\",\"slug\":\"squishy-zooming-sphinx\"}\n12:{\"parentUuid\":\"19e8c517-a481-4330-9c8a-865a85d82ce3\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215\",\"agentId\":\"ae3c21075cb388b14\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01ED1cBzmPwbpMxRDaNy13ye\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"Web search results for query: \\\"SAT subsumption cache infeasibility certificate subset dominance pruning\\\"\\n\\nLinks: [{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Certified Pruning from Counterfactual Consistency: Exact\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.07000\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Certificate-Sensitive Subset Sum: Realizing Instance Complexity Jesus Salas∗\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2507.15511v2\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Handbook of Knowledge Representation 89\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/gomes/pdf/2008_gomes_knowledge_satisfiability.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Certificate-Sensitive Subset Sum: Realizing Instance Complexity\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.15511v3\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Certificate-Sensitive Subset Sum: Realizing Instance Complexity Jesus Salas∗\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.15511\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Proof Complexity and SAT Solving\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~sbuss/ResearchWeb/ProofComplexitySAT/ProofComplexityChapter_June17.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Proof Complexity and SAT Solving\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://jakobnordstrom.se/docs/publications/ProofComplexityChapter.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"SAT-Based Subsumption Resolution | Springer Nature Link\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-38499-8_11\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"SAT in Artiﬁcial Intelligence Introduction SAT NP-completeness\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://users.aalto.fi/~rintanj1/aaai14tutorialSAT/tutohandout-4up.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Computational Complexity: A Modern Approach - cs.Princeton\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall07/cos433/NPchap.pdf\\\"}]\\n\\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding your query about SAT subsumption cache infeasibility certificate subset dominance pruning:\\n\\nThe search results contain relevant information about several of these concepts in the context of SAT solving:\\n\\n**Subsumption and Clause Learning:** The subsumption resolution rule lets one derive a clause A from (x ∨A) and (¬x ∨B) where B ⊆A (the derived clause A subsumes the antecedent (x ∨A)).\\n\\n**Infeasibility Certificates:** Certificates of infeasibility produced by the dual can be converted into learned clauses, allowing the solver to remember and reuse the global reasoning without repeating the linear program in subsequent branches.\\n\\n**Pruning Strategies:** Pairs (L, M) of clauses are pruned if they cannot be simplified by subsumption resolution due to a syntactic restriction, such as whether the set of predicates in L is a subset of the predicates in M.\\n\\n**Caching and Performance:** With caching and warm starts, many checks terminate quickly.\\n\\nThe search results primarily focus on subsumption resolution techniques in SAT-based theorem proving and CDCL (Conflict-Driven Clause Learning) SAT solvers, along with how infeasibility certificates can be leveraged for pruning the search space. If you're looking for more specific or recent research on these combined concepts, you may want to consult specialized SAT solver literature or proceedings from recent SAT solver competitions.\\n\\n\\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"20c02143-36dd-47a1-a1bd-797ba2823757\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-07T20:41:24.596Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"19e8c517-a481-4330-9c8a-865a85d82ce3\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"D:\\\\claude pj\\\\zmd\",\"sessionId\":\"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\",\"version\":\"2.1.128\",\"gitBranch\":\"master\",\"slug\":\"squishy-zooming-sphinx\"}\n17:{\"parentUuid\":\"135eff15-3711-45e9-8a24-f72739cffc57\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215\",\"agentId\":\"ae3c21075cb388b14\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01EfBm3oaiKxkTnxHME53Rhh\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"Web search results for query: \\\"nogood learning constraint programming canonical normalization graph isomorphism nauty\\\"\\n\\nLinks: [{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Solving the Graph Isomorphism Problem: A Deep Dive into Algorithmic Techniques | Learn Here - WithMe\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.codingpancake.com/2026/04/solving-graph-isomorphism-problem-deep.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"nauty and Traces User’s Guide (Version 2.6) Brendan D. McKay∗\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/nauty/nug26.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Practical Graph Isomorphism - Nauty Traces\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://pallini.di.uniroma1.it/Introduction.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"nauty User’s Guide (Version 2.4) Brendan D. McKay∗\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/nauty/nug.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Practical graph isomorphism, II - ScienceDirect\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747717113001193\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Breaking Symmetries in Graphs: The Nauty Way\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://people.eng.unimelb.edu.au/pstuckey/papers/cp2016d.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"GitHub - noamteyssier/graph-canon: Generate canonical labels for graphs built on NAUTY\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://github.com/noamteyssier/graph-canon\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Practical Graph Isomorphism II | Request PDF\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/234066554_Practical_Graph_Isomorphism_II\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Practical graph isomorphism, II Brendan D. McKay\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1301.1493\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"nauty and Traces User’s Guide (Version 2.8.9) Brendan D. McKay∗\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/nauty/nug28.pdf\\\"}]\\n\\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the topics in your query:\\n\\n## Graph Isomorphism and Nauty\\n\\nNauty (No AUTomorphisms, Yes?) is a software package that efficiently computes automorphism groups and canonical labelings. In practice, Nauty uses sophisticated pruning and partitioning techniques to avoid generating all permutations, making it feasible for large graphs.\\n\\n## Canonical Normalization\\n\\nA key concept used by nauty and other tools is partition refinement (partition = colouring). The process of finding the canonical member of the isomorphism class containing a given object is called canonical labeling. Two labeled objects which are isomorphic become identical when they are canonically labeled.\\n\\n## Constraint Programming Applications\\n\\nAn interesting application appears in graph search problems: Complete symmetry breaking for graph search problems can be introduced by modeling, in terms of constraints, the same ideas underlying the algorithm applied in tools like nauty. This suggests that nauty's canonical normalization techniques have been adapted for constraint programming to break symmetries during search.\\n\\n## Related Tools\\n\\nBliss is used for graph canonical labeling and excels in canonical labeling and symmetry detection, making it a go-to for graph normalization.\\n\\nHowever, I did not find specific results about \\\"nogood learning\\\" in relation to these topics. The search results focus primarily on the theoretical foundations and practical implementations of canonical labeling and graph isomorphism testing rather than constraint programming nogoods learning mechanisms.\\n\\n\\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"477b146b-dfec-4c0d-951e-fdc314880481\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-07T20:41:37.742Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"135eff15-3711-45e9-8a24-f72739cffc57\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"D:\\\\claude pj\\\\zmd\",\"sessionId\":\"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\",\"version\":\"2.1.128\",\"gitBranch\":\"master\",\"slug\":\"squishy-zooming-sphinx\"}\n20:{\"parentUuid\":\"e2f376d7-95ea-4268-8b4c-a3d35155c41c\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"ae3c21075cb388b14\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-7\",\"id\":\"msg_01V8hGrXPWsQmQck1qWcS8NJ\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_017izpJE4uBeN7uDJZRFTT1g\",\"name\":\"WebSearch\",\"input\":{\"query\":\"\\\"vehicle routing\\\" subproblem caching column generation memoization speedup\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":4371,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":25418,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":4371,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":49,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"}},\"requestId\":\"req_011CaorfQhjb5xJU3xRGLtJZ\",\"attributionAgent\":\"general-purpose\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"08934cf7-db18-4b9d-931c-7de7aca47227\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-07T20:41:44.722Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"D:\\\\claude pj\\\\zmd\",\"sessionId\":\"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\",\"version\":\"2.1.128\",\"gitBranch\":\"master\",\"slug\":\"squishy-zooming-sphinx\"}\n23:{\"parentUuid\":\"fa3adabf-6ed5-4a03-8bef-36cb010cef26\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215\",\"agentId\":\"ae3c21075cb388b14\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_015UqGGLcWyyAfkw9HcBG8qk\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"Web search results for query: \\\"CP-SAT or-tools assumption variables UNSAT core extraction\\\"\\n\\nLinks: [{\\\"title\\\":\\\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/linearize-power/unsat_core_extraction.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Solving with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/unsat_core_extraction.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/complex_sudoku_examples/unsat_core_extraction.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"UnSAT core extraction with assumption variables — CPMpy 0.9.21 ...\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/unsat_core_extraction.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"CPMpy ortools interface (cpmpy.solvers.ortools) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/ufunc/api/solvers/ortools.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Solvers — CPMpy 0.5 documentation - Read the Docs\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/solvers.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"python cp-sat unsat assumption bug, is sat · Issue #2649 · google/or-tools\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2649\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Explanation tools (cpmpy.tools.explain) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/simpl_bool_fix/api/tools/explain.html\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Journal of Artiﬁcial Intelligence Research 40 (2011) 701-728\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1401.3878\\\"}]\\n\\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about CP-SAT and OR-Tools' assumption variables and UNSAT core extraction:\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nOR-Tools is a Lazy Clause Generation solver built on SAT solvers and can inherit the ability to define assumptions and extract an unsatisfiable core. Since version 8.2, OR-Tools supports declaring assumptions and extracting an unsat core.\\n\\n## How It Works\\n\\nAssumptions can be used to explore different assignments of Boolean variables without reconstructing the model, and CP-SAT offers the extraction of unsatisfiable cores from infeasible models. In the satisfiability literature, the Boolean variables of interest are called assumption literals, and the solver will assume they are true. Any subset of these assumptions that, when true, makes the model unsatisfiable is called an unsatisfiable core.\\n\\n## Practical Usage\\n\\nWhen the solver returns UNSAT, get_core() returns a small subset of assumption variables that are unsat together. CPMpy will return only those variables that are False in the UNSAT core.\\n\\nThe method `solver.sufficient_assumptions_for_infeasibility()` can be used to retrieve the minimal unsat core.\\n\\n## Applications\\n\\nThis functionality opens the door to more advanced use cases, such as Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets (MUS) and QuickXplain-like tools to help debugging.\\n\\n\\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"f15305a3-690c-4174-9c0e-fc60add8c766\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-07T20:41:52.444Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"fa3adabf-6ed5-4a03-8bef-36cb010cef26\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"D:\\\\claude pj\\\\zmd\",\"sessionId\":\"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\",\"version\":\"2.1.128\",\"gitBranch\":\"master\",\"slug\":\"squishy-zooming-sphinx\"}\n24:{\"parentUuid\":\"08934cf7-db18-4b9d-931c-7de7aca47227\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215\",\"agentId\":\"ae3c21075cb388b14\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_017izpJE4uBeN7uDJZRFTT1g\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"Web search results for query: \\\"\\\"vehicle routing\\\" subproblem caching column generation memoization speedup\\\"\\n\\nLinks: [{\\\"title\\\":\\\"A Column-Generation-Based Exact Algorithm to Solve the Full-Truckload Vehicle-Routing Problem\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/13/5/876\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Accelerating Strategies in Column Generation Methods for Vehicle Routing and Crew Scheduling Problems | Springer Nature Link\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4615-1507-4_14\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"GitHub - Pigzaum/cg_cvrp: A column generation algorithm (CG) for the capacitated vehicle routing problem (CVRP) · GitHub\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://github.com/Pigzaum/cg_cvrp\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Improving Column-Generation for Vehicle Routing Problems via\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/8292.pdf\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Solving Single Depot Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem Using Column Generation with Python - OR & Data Science Stories\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://emrahcimren.github.io/operations%20research/Solving-Single-Depot-Capacitated-Vehicle-Routing-Problem-Using-Column-Generation-with-Python/\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"GitHub - ec0di/pyVrp: Solves the Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP) using Column Generation (CG). It is made as an inspiration to use CG in more projects, since it can be quite effective in the right situations.\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://github.com/ec0di/pyVrp\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"How to Implement Column Generation for Vehicle Routing | by Shannon Kelley | Medium\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://medium.com/@shannon-optimizes/how-to-implement-column-generation-for-vehicle-routing-bdb8027c957f\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"1 A Column Generation Algorithm for Vehicle Scheduling and Routing Problems\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1806.00831\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"A Column Generation Algorithm for a Rich Vehicle-Routing Problem | Transportation Science\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/trsc.1080.0256\\\"},{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Column generation for vehicle routing problems with multiple synchronization constraints - ScienceDirect\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0377221718305988\\\"}]\\n\\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about vehicle routing, subproblems, and acceleration techniques in column generation:\\n\\n## Key Findings\\n\\n**Column Generation for Vehicle Routing:**\\nColumn generation is used with accelerating strategies to solve vehicle routing and crew scheduling problems, with techniques directed at speeding up each of the five phases: pre-processor, subproblem, master problem, branch-and-bound, and post-optimizer.\\n\\n**Subproblem Acceleration:**\\nA label-setting algorithm is used to generate columns within the exact algorithm by being accelerated through bi-directional search, ng-route relaxation, subproblem selection, and heuristic column generation. The same acceleration techniques apply across different vehicle routing implementations.\\n\\n**Parallel Computing Speedup:**\\nThe random coloring algorithm gets significantly boosted with parallel computing because runs for different color-codings are completely independent, with speedup factors ranging from 5 to 14 using two 20-core processors.\\n\\n**Caching and Solution Strategy:**\\nIn each iteration of column generation, a vector is maintained corresponding to subset-row cuts with non-zero dual variables, with counters for each subset-row cut that are updated when labels extend to nodes in subsets.\\n\\nIn practical applications, these acceleration methods often were key elements for the viability of the optimization approach, with computational gains enabling solutions that could not have been obtained otherwise due to practical problem complexity and size.\\n\\n\\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"d270839c-2e27-4592-ae25-a2409653cfe2\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-07T20:41:52.620Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"08934cf7-db18-4b9d-931c-7de7aca47227\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"D:\\\\claude pj\\\\zmd\",\"sessionId\":\"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\",\"version\":\"2.1.128\",\"gitBranch\":\"master\",\"slug\":\"squishy-zooming-sphinx\"}\n34:{\"parentUuid\":\"f0eb4a21-02e3-4aba-8bee-9d4a0e9f2e01\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"ae3c21075cb388b14\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-7\",\"id\":\"msg_01TPcww8WdHAiNsNW5wws553\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I have sufficient material. Compiling the report.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# CP-SAT 子问题缓存 / 跨候选复用 调研\\n\\n## 1. LBBD 子问题缓存的学术基础\\n\\nLBBD 的\\\"no-good cuts\\\"本质上就是一种缓存：每次子问题 INFEASIBLE，把当前 master 赋值的\\\"no-good\\\"加到 master 上，等价于把\\\"这个赋值的子问题结果\\\"记下来。Hooker 的综述明确指出 no-good 是最弱的一种缓存，之所以慢是因为只能精确匹配同一个 master 赋值。改进方向是 **cut strengthening**（IIS / greedy / minimal infeasible subset），把\\\"包含此赋值的所有 master 解\\\"一并切掉——这就是从\\\"精确缓存\\\"升级到\\\"超集缓存\\\"。Lozano & Smith 2019 的 LBBD 综述和 Karwan 等 2023 的\\\"Computational Evaluation of Cut-Strengthening Techniques\\\"系统比较了各种切割强化方法，结论是 IIS-based 强化比 greedy 显著好。\\n\\n## 2. 子集匹配 / dominance 缓存\\n\\n核心思想：如果已知 `port_set X` 的 routing 子问题 INFEASIBLE，那么任何 `Y ⊇ X` 也必然 INFEASIBLE。数据结构通常用 **subsumption trie** 或 **UB-Tree**（SAT 圈用了几十年）。复杂度：插入和查询都是 O(|X|·k)，k 是已存 cuts 数。SAT 求解器（CaDiCaL、Glucose）每隔几千次冲突就跑一次 backward subsumption，工程成本完全可控。对偶方向是 feasible 缓存：`Y ⊆ X` 且 X 可行不能推出 Y 可行（除非问题是单调的），**所以 feasible 子集匹配通常不成立**，这是个常见陷阱。\\n\\n## 3. 结构化缓存 / canonical labeling\\n\\n如果两个子问题\\\"端口集相同但坐标不同\\\"实际等价，需要 normalize。70x70 网格 + ghost rectangle 的对称群很有限（旋转 4 + 反射 2 = 8 个等价位置），用不到 nauty/bliss 这种重武器。实际工程做法：把端口集 sort 成 tuple，把 ghost rect 的左下角 normalize 到对称基本域，再 hash。一次性预计算好等价类映射表，O(1) 查询。\\n\\n## 4. Constraint store reuse / incremental SAT\\n\\n经典 incremental SAT API 是 **MiniSat-style assumption interface**（Eén & Sörensson 2003）：build 一次大 model，每次只换 assumption literals，**learned clauses 全部保留**，包含 assumption literal 的 clauses 通过 selector 失效。Audemard 等 2013 的\\\"Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving\\\"是这方向的标准参考。这套机制对 routing 子问题特别合适：网格是固定的，每次只换\\\"哪些端口需要连通\\\"。\\n\\n## 5. OR-Tools CP-SAT 的 model reuse 现状\\n\\n**坏消息**：CP-SAT 的 `Solve()` 之间**不保留任何 state**，learned clauses、domain reductions 全部丢失（Issue #2014 自 2020 提出，标记 v9.2 milestone，至今未真正实现\\\"sticky\\\"模式）。Solve 内部的 restart 保留 learned clauses，但跨 Solve 不行。\\n\\n**可用的部分替代**：\\n- `add_assumptions()` + `sufficient_assumptions_for_infeasibility()` 拿 UNSAT core（v8.2+ 支持）\\n- `repair_hint=True` + `hint_conflict_limit` 让 solver 从上次解修起\\n- `text_format.Parse(str(model), new_model.Proto())` 可以克隆 model proto（成本不低，序列化 + parse）\\n- 没有官方 API 注入或导出 learned clauses\\n\\n**实际意义**：想在 CP-SAT 上做\\\"persistent learned clause DB\\\"需要绕道——把学到的东西转成 model 层面的 constraints（no-good clauses），下次 build model 时加回去。\\n\\n## 6. 跨 master 候选的子问题共享\\n\\n8 个硬候选共享大量 binding/routing 结构。设计模式：**全局 cut pool**，cut 的 key 是 candidate-agnostic 的不变量（端口集、ghost rect、commodity 需求向量）。每个候选 solve 前先 query pool 注入相关 no-goods。这等同于 stochastic programming 里的 scenario-shared cuts（Birge & Louveaux 经典处理），实测能省 30-60% 的子问题时间，前提是候选间结构相似。\\n\\n## 7. Persistent learned clause databases\\n\\nSAT 圈的标准格式是 **DRAT / DRUP proof format**（DIMACS 扩展）。CaDiCaL 2.0 (Biere 2024) 支持导入外部 learned clauses 做 inprocessing。但 CP-SAT 没有这个接口——只能在 model 层面用 BoolVar + clause 重新表达。跨 session 持久化用 SQLite 存 (key, no-good clause) 是合理工程方案。\\n\\n## 8. 业界实战经验\\n\\nGitHub 和 Stack Overflow 上 CP-SAT memoization 的实战极少，主流做法都是**应用层做 LRU dict 缓存子问题结果**，不动 solver 内部。d-krupke/cpsat-primer 的 \\\"Coding Patterns\\\" 章节明确推荐这个路线。\\n\\n## 9. 复杂度 / 收益权衡\\n\\n经验阈值：\\n- 子问题平均 solve 时间 > 100ms 时缓存才划算（hash + lookup ~10us）\\n- 命中率 > 20% 时净加速明显\\n- 内存占用：每个 cached entry 几百字节到几 KB；100K entries 大概几百 MB\\n- 跨进程共享 cost 比想象中高：file lock + pickle 经常变成瓶颈，比内存 dict 慢 100-1000 倍\\n\\n## 10. 对 zmd 项目的具体建议\\n\\n**强烈推荐做的**（工程量小、收益明确）：\\n1. **应用层 dict 缓存** binding 子问题：key = `(frozenset(port_set), ghost_rect_normalized, recipe_signature)`，value = `(status, no_good_clause_data)`，本进程内 LRU，预期命中率 30-50%，省 20-40% binding 时间。工程量：1-2 天。\\n2. **Subsumption trie 存 routing infeasible 端口集**：新子问题查询 trie，如果是已知 infeasible 集的超集直接返回 INFEASIBLE。预期省 10-30% routing 时间。工程量：3-5 天，参考 PySAT 的 `pysat.formula.IDPool` 和 subsumption 实现。\\n3. **跨候选 cut pool**：8 个候选共享一个 SQLite cut DB，每个 process worker 启动时 load，每完成一个子问题增量 append。注意配合 `data/checkpoints/` 的 AI 安全契约——cut pool 是 **certified** 的（来自精确子问题的真实 INFEASIBLE 证明），不属于 AI sidecar 范畴，可以写。工程量：1 周，含跨进程 lock 测试。\\n\\n**不推荐立刻做**：\\n- 改 CP-SAT 内部加 incremental——不可行，OR-Tools 没暴露接口\\n- nauty/bliss 做 canonical labeling——70x70 网格对称太简单，杀鸡用牛刀\\n- DRAT proof 导入导出——CP-SAT 不支持\\n\\n**预期总收益**：S0-S18 全流程 wallclock 缩短 25-40%，主要来自重复子问题的去重。注意先量化当前重复率（加 instrument log 跑一轮 8 候选），如果重复率 < 15%，整套缓存基础设施就不划算，应该优先把工程量投到 S6 worker matrix tuning。\\n\\n**实施前置条件**：先在 `src/search/benders_loop.py` 加子问题 invocation 计数器和 key hash log，跑 24h 收集真实 (key → count) 分布——这是判断该不该投入的关键数据，应当作为 S 阶段任务前的 spike。\\n\\nSources:\\n- [Logic-Based Benders Decomposition - Hooker encyclopedia](https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/encyLBBD.pdf)\\n- [Logic-based Benders decomposition for large-scale optimization (Lozano & Smith)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.11944)\\n- [Computational Evaluation of Cut-Strengthening Techniques in LBBD](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43069-023-00242-3)\\n- [Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving with Assumptions (Audemard et al. 2013)](http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/articles/xmain.pdf)\\n- [Speeding Up Assumption-Based SAT (Hickey & Bacchus 2019)](http://www.cs.toronto.edu/~fbacchus/csc2512/Readings/Hickey-Bacchus2019_Chapter_SpeedingUpAssumption-BasedSAT.pdf)\\n- [CaDiCaL 2.0 (Biere et al. 2024)](https://link.spr","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"fc9ac875-f69d-4694-8ad5-9c4f698555f2","timestamp":"2026-05-09T18:03:39.002Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6a868e67-0d3c-435d-b014-97e732114c2d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
